Pages = {1572-1593},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {When considering how hotels can facilitate aesthetics that offer
   restorative benefits to business and leisure travelers, extant research
   suggests natural environments serve as a refuge and that time spent in
   nature promotes well-being. Based on the tenets of Stress Recovery
   Theory (SRT), combined with the theoretical perspective of
   prospect-refuge, we examine, through a series of conjoint analyses, how
   combinations of biophilic design elements differ in the extent to which
   they are perceived to enhance guests' restoration, including relaxation,
   energy, and mind clarity. We further explore how these perceptions
   change based on higher and lower levels of individuals' longer-term
   states of anxiety and stress. Findings can be translated into practical
   applications, ensuring that the biophilic elements more closely aligned
   with restoration and psychological needs are prioritized in urban hotel
   building and interior design.},
